  Notes on reproducibility
  The random datasets are generated once, from a fixed seed ( set.seed(731969) ), so
  every run uses the same 1,000 datasets per driver.

  Re-running the estimation from scratch reproduces exactly all WARP results, the verdicts
  on the drivers' actual choices, the proximities (Table A2), Table A1, Table A5 and the
  Section 5.6 counts. Results that depend on area rates (the Selten measures in Table A3
  and the figures that plot them, and the paired tests in Table A4) can differ very slightly.
  The cause is the linear-program solver (lpSolve 5.6.19). On a small number of numerically
  ill-conditioned random datasets it stops without a verdict, mostly with a numerical failure
  (status 5), and the code records the dataset as unresolved rather than as a pass. Whether
  a given borderline program fails in this way is not perfectly repeatable: it can vary
  between runs, machines, operating systems and package versions. Raising the solver's
  time limit does not help, because these failures occur within seconds, not at the limit.

  When a dataset that is unresolved in one run is resolved in another and passes, the area
  rate for that driver and model changes by 0.001 (one dataset in 1,000), and the Selten
  measure by the same amount. A from-scratch rerun on a different machine may therefore
  change a few Selten measures in Table A3 by 0.001 and the paired tests in Table A4 in the
  fourth decimal place, with no change in sign or significance. The number of unresolved
  solves in Section 5.5 also varies between runs: the run reported in the paper gave 421
  (Both dimension), and the shipped results files, from a second from-scratch run, give 375.
  All such differences lie within the worst-case bound in Section 5.5, which treats every
  unresolved dataset as passing: no area rate rises by more than 2.9 percentage points and
  no reported comparison reverses.




